1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,653
Usually, we get an equation
and find its roots.

2
00:00:04,053 --> 00:00:06,210
Ravi turned it around for Aarav.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:08,220
I am thinking of a quadratic.

4
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,133
Its roots are two and five.

5
00:00:10,133 --> 00:00:11,588
What is my equation?

6
00:00:12,038 --> 00:00:15,159
Aarav had it on his study
table in ten seconds.

7
00:00:15,509 --> 00:00:17,132
Then Ravi pushed harder.

8
00:00:17,132 --> 00:00:19,919
Roots one plus two i and one minus two i.

9
00:00:19,919 --> 00:00:23,291
Can you still build it,
with no i in the equation?

10
00:00:23,791 --> 00:00:24,691
Yes.

11
00:00:24,319 --> 00:00:28,497
Today we build quadratic equations
backwards, from their roots.

12
00:00:29,147 --> 00:00:31,169
Start with the simplest idea.

13
00:00:31,169 --> 00:00:34,929
A product is zero only when
one of its factors is zero.

14
00:00:35,279 --> 00:00:40,537
So x minus two, times x minus five,
equals zero, is true when x is two,

15
00:00:40,537 --> 00:00:42,127
and when x is five.

16
00:00:42,127 --> 00:00:43,209
Nowhere else.

17
00:00:43,609 --> 00:00:44,949
Multiply it out.

18
00:00:44,949 --> 00:00:48,262
x squared minus seven
x plus ten equals zero.

19
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:50,049
That is Ravi's equation.

20
00:00:50,699 --> 00:00:54,605
Now let the roots move, and watch
the equation rebuild itself.

21
00:00:54,605 --> 00:00:57,047
The roots stay equally far from three.

22
00:00:57,447 --> 00:00:58,887
Roots one and five.

23
00:00:58,887 --> 00:01:01,717
Their sum is six, their product is five.

24
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:04,966
The equation is x squared
minus six x plus five.

25
00:01:05,366 --> 00:01:06,760
Slide them together.

26
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,315
The sum stays six, the product grows.

27
00:01:09,715 --> 00:01:11,274
Roots three and three.

28
00:01:11,274 --> 00:01:12,735
The product is nine.

29
00:01:12,735 --> 00:01:17,129
x squared minus six x plus nine,
which is x minus three, squared.

30
00:01:17,529 --> 00:01:19,532
Now step off the number line.

31
00:01:19,532 --> 00:01:21,691
Three plus i and three minus i.

32
00:01:21,691 --> 00:01:24,648
The product is nine
plus one, which is ten.

33
00:01:25,048 --> 00:01:29,536
At three plus two i and three minus
two i, the product is thirteen.

34
00:01:29,536 --> 00:01:34,330
The equation is x squared minus six
x plus thirteen, with no i in sight.

35
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:35,880
Quick one.

36
00:01:35,842 --> 00:01:38,140
The roots are four and minus one.

37
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:39,973
What is the constant term?

38
00:01:39,973 --> 00:01:41,191
Pause and think.

39
00:01:44,391 --> 00:01:45,375
Minus four!

40
00:01:45,375 --> 00:01:50,505
The constant is the product of the
roots, and four times minus one is minus

41
00:01:50,505 --> 00:01:51,405
four.

42
00:01:51,104 --> 00:01:54,738
The equation is x squared
minus three x minus four.

43
00:01:55,388 --> 00:01:57,915
Now the general rule, in small steps.

44
00:01:57,915 --> 00:01:59,950
Call the roots alpha and beta.

45
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:01,250
Step one.

46
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:04,829
x minus alpha, times x
minus beta, equals zero.

47
00:02:04,829 --> 00:02:07,833
It is zero exactly at alpha and at beta.

48
00:02:08,583 --> 00:02:09,513
Step two.

49
00:02:09,513 --> 00:02:10,671
Multiply out.

50
00:02:10,671 --> 00:02:15,221
x squared, minus alpha x,
minus beta x, plus alpha beta.

51
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:16,930
Step three.

52
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:18,640
Group the middle terms.

53
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:23,728
x squared minus alpha plus beta,
times x, plus alpha beta, equals zero.

54
00:02:24,478 --> 00:02:25,378
Step four.

55
00:02:25,373 --> 00:02:30,712
In words: x squared, minus the sum
times x, plus the product, equals zero.

56
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:36,074
This is the last episode, run backwards:
with a equal to one, the middle

57
00:02:36,074 --> 00:02:38,000
coefficient is minus the sum.

58
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:41,644
But is it the only
equation with those roots?

59
00:02:41,644 --> 00:02:42,708
Side by side.

60
00:02:43,108 --> 00:02:45,445
x squared minus seven x plus ten.

61
00:02:45,445 --> 00:02:48,407
Its curve crosses the
axis at two and five.

62
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:51,597
Two x squared minus
fourteen x plus twenty.

63
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:53,477
Every coefficient is doubled.

64
00:02:53,477 --> 00:02:57,420
The curve is steeper, but it
crosses at the same two points.

65
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:02,269
So any non zero number k times
the equation has the same roots.

66
00:03:02,269 --> 00:03:05,775
That is why we say a
quadratic, not the quadratic.

67
00:03:06,175 --> 00:03:07,122
Your turn.

68
00:03:07,122 --> 00:03:12,127
Does minus x squared plus seven x
minus ten equal zero have roots two and

69
00:03:12,127 --> 00:03:13,027
five?

70
00:03:12,737 --> 00:03:14,129
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:17,329 --> 00:03:18,229
Yes!

72
00:03:17,848 --> 00:03:20,410
It is minus one times Ravi's equation.

73
00:03:20,410 --> 00:03:23,968
The curve flips, but still
crosses at two and five.

74
00:03:24,618 --> 00:03:25,656
Remember this.

75
00:03:25,656 --> 00:03:29,991
x squared, minus the sum times
x, plus the product, equals zero.

76
00:03:30,691 --> 00:03:33,910
Now, Ravi's second challenge
as a worked example.

77
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:38,904
Form a quadratic with real coefficients
whose roots are one plus two i

78
00:03:38,904 --> 00:03:40,567
and one minus two i.

79
00:03:41,067 --> 00:03:41,967
Step one.

80
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:45,323
The sum is one plus two
i plus one minus two i.

81
00:03:45,323 --> 00:03:48,602
The imaginary parts
cancel, so the sum is two.

82
00:03:49,352 --> 00:03:50,252
Step two.

83
00:03:50,198 --> 00:03:53,595
The product is one squared
minus two i, squared.

84
00:03:53,595 --> 00:03:57,642
That is one minus four i
squared, which is one plus four.

85
00:03:57,642 --> 00:03:58,542
Five.

86
00:03:58,970 --> 00:03:59,884
Step three.

87
00:03:59,884 --> 00:04:00,907
Put them in.

88
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:03,878
x squared minus two x
plus five equals zero.

89
00:04:04,628 --> 00:04:06,565
Every coefficient is real.

90
00:04:06,565 --> 00:04:10,352
A conjugate pair always
gives a real sum and product.

91
00:04:11,002 --> 00:04:12,468
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:12,818 --> 00:04:14,219
Roots two and five.

93
00:04:14,219 --> 00:04:18,589
Many students write x squared plus
seven x plus ten: the wrong sign in

94
00:04:18,589 --> 00:04:19,489
the middle.

95
00:04:19,884 --> 00:04:22,584
Its roots are minus two and minus five.

96
00:04:22,584 --> 00:04:24,922
The middle term is minus the sum.

97
00:04:24,922 --> 00:04:28,509
The right answer is x squared
minus seven x plus ten.

98
00:04:28,509 --> 00:04:30,322
Always check with a root.

99
00:04:30,973 --> 00:04:32,422
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:32,422 --> 00:04:37,305
In JEE Main: build a new equation from
the roots of an old one, as in April

101
00:04:37,305 --> 00:04:38,647
twenty twenty four.

102
00:04:38,647 --> 00:04:41,108
It is not in the current NCERT text.

103
00:04:41,708 --> 00:04:42,911
Exam level now.

104
00:04:42,911 --> 00:04:46,060
Alpha and beta are the
roots of this equation.

105
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:49,984
Which equation has the roots
alpha squared and beta squared?

106
00:04:49,984 --> 00:04:51,237
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:56,637 --> 00:04:57,997
The answer is A.

108
00:04:58,497 --> 00:05:02,976
For the old equation, alpha plus
beta is three and alpha beta is one.

109
00:05:03,476 --> 00:05:04,376
New sum.

110
00:05:04,288 --> 00:05:09,332
Alpha squared plus beta squared is
the sum squared, minus two alpha beta.

111
00:05:09,332 --> 00:05:11,717
Nine minus two, which is seven.

112
00:05:12,217 --> 00:05:13,264
New product.

113
00:05:13,264 --> 00:05:17,299
Alpha squared beta squared
is alpha beta, squared: one.

114
00:05:17,299 --> 00:05:21,087
So the equation is x squared
minus seven x plus one.

115
00:05:21,687 --> 00:05:23,146
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:23,146 --> 00:05:24,646
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:27,846 --> 00:05:30,682
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:31,082 --> 00:05:31,982
One.

119
00:05:31,596 --> 00:05:35,171
Form a quadratic whose roots
are three and minus four.

120
00:05:36,071 --> 00:05:36,971
Two.

121
00:05:36,644 --> 00:05:40,675
Why does a conjugate pair
always give real coefficients?

122
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:42,475
Three.

123
00:05:42,163 --> 00:05:45,964
Write two different quadratics
whose roots are both minus two.

124
00:05:47,064 --> 00:05:48,965
That completes Chapter 4.

125
00:05:48,965 --> 00:05:52,954
Every episode is in the PUC
Maths Class eleven playlist.
